sit_013:n1.prompt:
You are helping me respond to a termination notice from my payment processor. This step is about reading it carefully before anything else happens, because the stated reason and final processing date set every downstream deadline. Misreading the reserve terms is how founders end up planning payroll against money that is actually held.

Please ask me for anything missing:
- The full text of the termination notice (email or dashboard message), including the date I received it.
- Which processor it is, and the version or date of the services agreement my account is under.
- My current balance and pending payouts, if I can see them.

Then do the following:
1. Extract from the notice, quoting it exactly:
   - the stated reason for termination;
   - the final processing date and time, with time zone;
   - any stated reserve or payout-hold amount and duration;
   - any appeal or review channel mentioned, with its deadline.
2. Read the reserve and termination sections of my services agreement. If it is Stripe, the agreement is public at https://stripe.com/legal/ssa. Quote the clauses that govern holding funds against future disputes, refunds or chargebacks after termination. Flag any place where the notice and the agreement differ.
3. Build a dated timeline from today through the end of the reserve period. Mark the last day of API and dashboard access if the notice states it. Clearly label anything inferred rather than stated.
4. List the open questions I should put to the processor, such as an unclear reserve duration or a vague reason.

Do not contact the processor, file an appeal, or change any account settings. Do not decide what the business should do about the held funds. Once the summary is drafted, stop for human sign-off.

Report back with:
- a one-paragraph plain summary;
- the quoted extracts with their sources;
- the timeline table;
- the open questions;
- a short note on how much money appears held, and until when, so I can plan cash.
